{
 "title": "hazel-jit: Livelit Views Compiled to JavaScript",
 "date": "2026-11-06",
 "kind": "measurement, work in progress",
 "status": "work in progress",
 "question": "How much faster can a livelit's view run as JavaScript compiled from its Hazel, instead of on Hazel's interpreter? The best case first, JS's numbers allowed to differ. Decided at the meeting with Cyrus, 2026-11-05 (^ticket(694)); item 2 of the 2026-11-11 agenda (^report(42)).",
 "measurements": "Test_HazelJit's slow tests, Node 22.23.2, a development build of trial/694-view-jit at 97ba46706b, a Framework 13 laptop (i7-1370P), 2026-11-07 07:27 MST: each program 30 times on the interpreter and 30 times compiled after compiling once, performance.now; medians are the upper middle of the sorted runs. The Polygons render through the boundary: each series after 10 untimed runs, but for its first 30 renders, timed cold; its three steps timed alone (the model in, the call, the answer back), with a census of what each trip carries; the ids both ways; what the Hazel answer costs after the trip back (the command, HazelDOM's drawing, to_raw), and the same drawing straight from JS. The way back as a diff: lists of 10 to 10,000 points, the middle one changed, 15 runs after 5 untimed. Each run's reading is in timings.json.",
